# Score Transcripts

This notebook replays saved support conversations and scores two things
separately: whether the agent escalated when it should, and whether the
conversation ended with the expected resolution. One transcript has no final
answer, so its resolution score fails while its escalation score still
counts. The notebook runs offline and needs no credentials.

## Setup

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:tune, "~> 0.5.0"}
])
```

## Save the transcripts

Each row stores the conversation as an ordered list of turns in `output`.
`input` holds the ticket and the resolution the support team expects.
`expected` says whether the ticket should be escalated.

```elixir
transcripts = [
  %{
    id: "refund",
    input: %{ticket: "refund", resolution: "Refund sent"},
    expected: %{escalate: true},
    output: [
      %{role: :customer, text: "I'd like a refund"},
      %{role: :agent, text: "Escalating to payments"},
      %{role: :agent, text: "Refund sent"}
    ]
  },
  %{
    id: "password",
    input: %{ticket: "password reset", resolution: "Reset link sent"},
    expected: %{escalate: false},
    output: [
      %{role: :customer, text: "I can't log in"},
      %{role: :agent, text: "Reset link sent"}
    ]
  },
  %{
    id: "blocked",
    input: %{ticket: "blocked payment", resolution: "Payment restored"},
    expected: %{escalate: true},
    output: [
      %{role: :customer, text: "Payment is blocked"},
      %{role: :agent, text: "Escalating to payments"}
    ]
  }
]
```

The `blocked` conversation stops after the hand-off, so it has no final
answer.

## Define the scorers

Each scorer is a module with a `score/2` function that receives the case and
the saved turns. Keep the scorers separate so one can fail without hiding the
other.

`Escalation` checks whether any agent turn escalates and compares that with
`expected.escalate`.

```elixir
defmodule Escalation do
  @moduledoc "Scores whether the agent escalated exactly when the ticket required it."

  def score(row, turns) do
    escalated? = Enum.any?(turns, &escalation?/1)
    if escalated? == row.expected.escalate, do: 1, else: 0
  end

  def escalation?(%{role: :agent, text: "Escalating" <> _}), do: true
  def escalation?(_turn), do: false
end
```

`Resolution` reads the last turn. If the conversation ends on a customer turn
or an escalation, there is no final answer to judge, so the scorer returns an
error instead of a zero.

```elixir
defmodule Resolution do
  @moduledoc "Scores whether the final agent turn delivers the expected resolution."

  def score(row, turns) do
    case List.last(turns) do
      %{role: :agent, text: text} = turn ->
        cond do
          Escalation.escalation?(turn) -> {:error, :missing_final_answer}
          text == row.input.resolution -> 1
          true -> 0
        end

      _turn ->
        {:error, :missing_final_answer}
    end
  end
end
```

## Replay and evaluate

`Tune.replay/1` turns each row into a case. The saved turns reach the scorers
unchanged and in order.

```elixir
{:ok, report} =
  transcripts
  |> Tune.replay()
  |> Tune.score(:escalation, Escalation)
  |> Tune.score(:resolution, Resolution)
  |> Tune.evaluate()

for name <- [:escalation, :resolution] do
  summary = report.summaries[name]
  IO.puts("#{name}: mean #{summary.mean}, count #{summary.count}, coverage #{Float.round(summary.coverage, 3)}")
end
```

```text
escalation: mean 1.0, count 3, coverage 1.0
resolution: mean 1.0, count 2, coverage 0.667
```

Escalation scored every transcript. Resolution scored two of three. Its mean
covers only the scored rows, and coverage shows the missing one.

## Inspect row errors

Each observation keeps the scores that succeeded and the errors for the ones
that failed.

```elixir
for observation <- report.observations do
  IO.puts(
    "#{observation.id} #{observation.status} scores: #{inspect(observation.scores)} " <>
      "errors: #{inspect(observation.score_errors)}"
  )
end
```

```text
refund ok scores: %{resolution: 1, escalation: 1} errors: %{}
password ok scores: %{resolution: 1, escalation: 1} errors: %{}
blocked error scores: %{escalation: 1} errors: %{resolution: :missing_final_answer}
```

The `blocked` row has status `:error` because one of its scores failed. Its
escalation score is still in the summary.

The saved turns are kept on each observation, in their original order.

```elixir
refund = hd(report.observations)
IO.inspect(Enum.map(refund.output, & &1.role))
```

```text
[:customer, :agent, :agent]
```

